# config/kernel/Kconfig.ipc — IPC, the syscall entry and capabilities

config IPC_FASTPATH
    bool "IPC fast path: a call or reply to a parked peer skips the dispatch"
    default y
    help
      Chain E stage (a), crates/core/syscall/src/fastpath.rs. A fast call
      (SYS_IPC_FAST_CALL_EP) or reply-and-accept (SYS_IPC_FAST_REPLY_ACCEPT)
      whose peer is parked in the matching wait is finished from the trap
      entry, after the syscall filter, without the syscall table: the call
      writes its record as already ACCEPTED and leaves its handle in the
      server's per-task-slot deposit, the reply writes the answer into the
      record, and either switches straight to the peer it claimed. The
      peer's resume finds the deposit (or the answer) and skips the accept,
      the collect retry loop and the endpoint queue. Any precondition that
      does not hold (a capability moved, a donation needed, calls already
      queued, the peer not parked, preemption disabled, a kill pending)
      leaves the call to the ordinary path, unchanged. With WAIT_GRAPH the
      fast call records the caller's wait-graph edge as the ordinary call
      does, and only when it stays lazy (no boost, no chain, no cycle check:
      no graph lock); a call the graph must act on, and a reply to one, take
      the ordinary path.
      Runtime canary `canary=ipc-force-slow` turns it off for one boot.
      Cost: 16 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS: the deposit word and two
      per-task hit counters), 1,024 B at edge's 64 tasks. Instructions,
      one CPU, vsbench ipc-roundtrip (one call and its reply): riscv64
      2,390 -> 1,933, aarch64 2,128 -> 1,659, x86_64 2,236 -> 1,843
      (the ordinary path, canary ipc-force-slow: 2,427 / 2,148 / 2,232).
      With the class dispatch as well (and resume
      points): 2,416 -> 1,852, 2,223 -> 1,592, 2,274 -> 1,782.

config IPC_FASTPATH_RESUME
    bool "IPC fast path: finish a parked peer's syscall and start it at its exit"
    depends on IPC_FASTPATH
    default y
    help
      Chain E commit 9, crates/core/syscall/src/fastpath.rs ("Resume
      points"). A task that blocks in the fast path's hand-off leaves a
      resume point: its trap frame and its ISA's resume stub. The fast call
      or reply that claims it next finishes its syscall for it under the
      endpoint lock, as seL4's fast path does: the request or the answer goes
      straight into its registers (no deposit, no REPLIED record to collect;
      a call with a donation still goes through the record), and its saved
      context is pointed at the stub, which leaves through the syscall exit
      without returning through the hand-off, the fast path, the dispatch and
      the trap handler, whether the claim switches to it or queues it on
      another hart. Any other wake (an ordinary call or reply, a drain, a
      kill, the scheduler) resumes it where it blocked, as before; so does a
      claim while syscall tracing is on, Linux signal work is pending or the
      task has an exec hand-off. Compiled out with `switch-census` and
      `ipc-census`. Runtime canaries `canary=ipc-resume-off` (no resume point
      is left) and `canary=ipc-resume-stale` (a task resumed where it blocked
      is left holding one). Cost: 16 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS),
      1,024 B at 64 tasks. Instructions, vsbench ipc-roundtrip, one CPU:
      riscv64 1,778 -> 1,657, aarch64 1,544 -> 1,449, x86_64 1,693 -> 1,637;
      riscv64 with `ipc-resume-off` 1,807, with this option off 1,783.
      Client and server pinned to two harts (riscv64, -smp 2): 8,158 with the
      option off -> 7,994.

config IPC_RESOLVE_CACHE
    bool "IPC: per-task cache of the last endpoint a fast call resolved"
    default y
    help
      Chain E commit 5, crates/core/ipc/src/endpoint.rs (resolve_cache).
      SYS_IPC_FAST_CALL_EP (the fast path and the ordinary arm) and
      SYS_IPC_CALL_V2 resolve their endpoint capability through a per-task
      slot entry: the capability handle, the capability slot word and the
      endpoint's identity word a full resolution accepted. A call hits when
      both words are unchanged (so a revoke, a re-grant, a rights change, an
      endpoint destroyed, reused or served by another task all miss on their
      own) and containment is off; any miss is the full
      resolution, with its denial recording. Cleared at task exit and exec.
      Cost: 32 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS), 2,048 B at 64 tasks.

config IPC_LABELS
    bool "IPC: provenance labels, and the effect gate's meet over them"
    default y if !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    help
      Innovation B4, crates/core/ipc/src/endpoint.rs (label). Every task has
      a principal (the kind its SCHED.TOML class declares: system, service,
      user, agent) and a scope (the label of the data it works on); a message
      carries its sender's scope with the sender's principal ORed in, on the
      v2 descriptor and on the register fast path alike. A server works in
      the scope of the request it accepted, a caller absorbs the label of
      every answer, and the effect gate holds a reversible effect to the
      meet (the most restrictive) of the envelopes of the kinds in the label
      of the task that issues it. A one-shot DECLASSIFY warrant, minted by a
      task whose principal is user, takes bits off one message.
      Off: no task has a principal or a scope, a descriptor whose label word
      is not 0 is refused (-EINVAL), no DECLASSIFY warrant can be minted,
      the gate holds an effect to nothing but its own record and deadline,
      and the hooks on the send, accept, reply and fast paths compile to
      nothing. Not on a small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED): its topology has one
      class per device and no agent, so there is no principal to tell apart.
      Cost: 8 B of RAM per task slot (MAX_TASKS: principal and scope), 4 B
      per call record (the label beside the words), 4 B per principal kind,
      and a few loads and one OR on the send, accept and reply of an IPC.

config SYSCALL_LEAN_ENTRY
    bool "Native syscalls save only what the native ABI preserves"
    default y
    help
      Chain E commit 8. The native syscall ABI (crates/core/abi/src/
      syscall_nr.rs, "Register contract"): on return from a native syscall
      ring 3 may rely only on the callee-saved registers, sp/gp/tp (and
      their per-ISA equivalents), the link register and the call's output
      registers; every other register is clobbered. With this on, a native
      task's syscall takes a trap-entry path that does not store the
      clobbered registers and returns them zeroed: riscv64 t0-t6, a7;
      aarch64 x10-x17; x86_64 rcx and r11, which `sysret` loads with the
      return RIP and RFLAGS. A Linux-personality task keeps the full save and restore
      (riscv64/aarch64 tag its trap frame at its first entry to user mode;
      the x86_64 Linux ABI clobbers rcx/r11 too), and so does every trap
      that is not a syscall. Off: every syscall saves and restores the whole
      register file, as before. The contract, and the clobbers every ring-3
      stub declares, are the same either way. Instructions, one CPU, vsbench
      (before -> after, the stubs' clobbers included): syscall-floor riscv64
      180 -> 168, aarch64 152 -> 146, x86_64 139 -> 127; ipc-roundtrip
      1,794 -> 1,778, 1,549 -> 1,544, 1,717 -> 1,693.

config IPC_PORT_NOTICES
    bool "Endpoint no-senders and source-gone notices on event ports"
    default y
    help
      Counts, per endpoint, the send capabilities that exist anywhere (a
      Cap<Endpoint> with WRITE and without READ), kept exact through every
      grant, move, revoke, fork copy and the table wipe at a task's exit. A
      server that binds its endpoint to a port (PORT_BIND, source
      4) gets PORT_EVENT_NO_SENDERS (code ENOSENDERS) when the count falls
      to zero. A channel, io_ring, endpoint or IRQ bound to a port that is
      destroyed, or whose owner dies, reports one PORT_EVENT_SOURCE_GONE
      (code EREVOKED or EPEERDIED) instead of leaving its waiter waiting.

      n: nothing is counted or reported; PORT_BIND refuses
      source 4 (an endpoint) with -EINVAL, and a gone source stays bound
      and silent, as before.

      Cost: 8 B of RAM per endpoint (IPC_ENDPOINTS) and 2 B per task slot
      (MAX_TASKS); on a capability grant, revoke or move a compare of the
      slot's kind (an atomic add when it is a send capability); a task
      exit whose table holds a send capability walks its used slots once.
      The fast call itself is untouched (a call that moves no capability
      runs no capability operation).

config CAP_SLOT_EXT
    bool "Capability slots are 128 bits: badge and rights width"
    default y
    help
      Each capability slot gets a second 64-bit word beside the one every
      check reads (crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs, CapExt): the badge a server
      sees on a v2 call through an endpoint capability (ext_of), and a
      reserved field (eight more rights bits). The parent link and the
      expiry live in the slot's warrant (WARRANT_DEPTH_MAX). Two words make
      the slot the 128 bits of a CHERI capability (owner Q9). The v1 fast
      call never reads the second word.

      n: slots stay one word, every capability reads as unbadged, and
      minting a badged endpoint capability (CapTable::mint_badged) is
      refused, so a v2 server cannot tell its clients apart by badge. The
      kernel-hosted device interfaces name their resource by badge (a pin,
      an I2C address, a PWM channel, a wheel), so without this a topology
      row for a device other than resource 0 is not granted.

      Cost: 8 B of RAM per slot per task slot, as much again as
      MAX_CAPS_PER_TASK costs: embedded (32 x 32) 8 KiB, edge (256 x 64)
      128 KiB, fleet (512 x 4096) 16 MiB; the same on every ISA. A grant
      pays one more store and a fence; a v2 call one more load and a fence.
      Turn it off on a board whose RAM cannot carry it and whose servers do
      not need badges.

config NAMES_LEND_TTL_MS
    int "Lifetime of a capability the name server lends (ms, 0 = the server's own)"
    range 0 86400000
    default 0 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 600000 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 3600000
    help
      The name server (NAMESD.ELF) stamps every endpoint it lends with
      `now + this`: the transfer descriptor's `expiry_ns`, which the kernel
      turns into the deadline of the borrower's copy in the warrant tree (the
      earlier of it and the server's own, so a lease never outlives its
      parent). When it passes, a call through the copy fails as stale and the
      borrower asks the name server again (azos_names::Renewing). 0 lends
      with no deadline of its own: the copy lives as long as the server's
      capability, which a static wiring (EMBEDDED) has no use to shorten.

      A shorter lease bounds how long a revoked or re-routed service stays
      reachable through an old copy; each renewal is one more name-server
      call per lease, so a very short one costs a lookup every few calls.
      Cost: no RAM; one clock read per lookup. The same on every ISA: it is a
      wall-clock lifetime, not a cycle count.

config CAP_WEAK
    bool "Weak (transitively read-only) capabilities for shadow rows"
    default y
    help
      A topology task row with `shadow = true` (CAPS.TOML format 7) is
      admitted in observe-only mode: its capability table is marked weak
      (crates/core/ipc/src/cap_store.rs, mark_weak), so every capability it
      holds, however it came by it, resolves with its rights masked to READ,
      and everything derived from one stays weak (Warrant::WEAK, which no
      derive can clear). Each use the mask refuses is recorded as a
      shadow-refusal decision (DECISION_RECORDS_DEV), the evidence for
      granting the authority by editing the signed row. The mask itself is
      one flag test in the slot word every resolve already loads.

      n: no weak warrant is ever published and a shadow row is seeded with
      no capabilities at all (it cannot run strong).
